1. Purpose
Daniel is on the team for six months (2026-11-12 to mid-May 2027). This page proposes which phases of AUTO-188 and AUTO-56 he can finish in that time, why, and what each one needs from other people. It is a planning guide, not a commitment. Eric decides the order and the approvals.

2. Summary
Commit: AUTO-188 Phases 6, 9 and 7, closing the gaps in Phases 1 and 3, and AUTO-56 Phases 0 and 1.
Stretch: AUTO-188 Phase 2 (US half only) and AUTO-56 Phase 3, the first Velocity release.
Not planned in six months: AUTO-188 Phases 4, 5 and 8, and AUTO-56 Phases 2 and 4 to 9. Each waits on a decision or approval that is not Daniel's (hosting, model approval, viewer platform, Jira administrator work).
Build effort: 79-135 engineer hours for the commit list, plus two items the tickets do not estimate (metric records and the Confluence Cloud client). The stretch items add 44-72 hours.
What limits the plan: answers and approvals from other people, more than the build hours.
3. Assumptions
Capacity is about 2 to 3 days a week on this work, roughly 250-400 build hours over six months. This is an assumption. Daniel confirms his real allocation.
Hours are the engineer ranges from the tickets, except where marked "planning estimate".
The tickets assumed a build from nothing. Phases 1, 3 and 6 start from working code, so some ranges may be high. The gaps the tickets list that the slices do not cover (metric records, the Cloud client, the team-lead notifier) are not in the ranges.
The ticket figures are judgment estimates, not measurements. Eric has said the AUTO-56 figures look high, and its Phase 0 re-baselines them.
Time saved per year is the ticket's figure and is mostly Eric's estimate.
